          "body": "Route rule keys can now be scoped to a named request condition with `METHOD:NAME /path`. Conditions match request headers and are defined in `routeRules()` options.\n\n```ts\nimport { routeRules } from \"h3/rules\";\n\napp.use(\n  routeRules(\n    {\n      // GET /blog/hello with `Accept: text/markdown` → 307 /blog/hello.md\n      \"GET:MD /blog/**\": { redirect: \"/blog/**.md\" },\n      // Already Markdown: don't redirect again\n      \"GET:MD /blog/*.md\": { redirect: false },\n    },\n    {\n      conditions: {\n        MD: { headers: { accept: /\\btext\\/markdown\\b/ } },\n      },\n    },\n  ),\n);\n```\n\n- A request that satisfies a condition keeps every plain `GET` rule and gets the condition's rules on top. When several conditions are satisfied, all of them apply.\n- Header checks: `true` means present, `false` means absent, a string must match exactly, and a `RegExp` is tested against the value.\n- Rule sets without conditions do no extra work per request.\n- `cache` entries and memoized matches are kept separate per condition.\n- The compiler supports conditions: `compileRouteRules(config, { conditions })` exports `resolveRouteRulesMethod(event, method)` for the compiled matcher.\n\nAlso fixes a `preMerge` bug where a method-scoped rule on a shared route node (`GET /a/:id`) hid an agnostic rule (`/a/*`) for `GET /a`.\n\n🤖 Generated with AI assistant\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n- **New Features**\n  - Added named request conditions for route rules, matching requests by header presence, absence, exact values, or regular expressions.\n  - Requests now combine standard method rules with rules for satisfied conditions. GET conditions also apply to HEAD requests.\n  - Added validation for condition definitions and rule keys, including a limit of six conditions per method.\n  - Cached responses are kept separate across condition variants.\n- **Documentation**\n  - Expanded the Route Rules guide with configuration, matching behavior, precedence, and security considerations.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->",
